(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_biotechnologyprinciplesandprocesses=[{id:"neet-2026-q098",pn:98,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction endonucleases",q:`Which of the following statements are not true regarding restriction endonucleases?
A. They are called molecular scissors.
B. These are the enzymes responsible for restricting the growth of bacteriophages in E. coli.
C. They cut the DNA only at the centre of the palindromic sites.
D. They remove nucleotides only from the ends of DNA fragments.
E. They recognise specific palindromic base-pair sequences.`,o:["A and B only","D and E only","C and D only","A and E only"],c:2,s:`Statements C and D are incorrect.
Restriction endonucleases usually cut the DNA slightly away from the centre of the palindrome sites.
They cannot remove nucleotides from the ends of a DNA fragment; that is the job of restriction exonuclease.`},{id:"neet-2026-q110",pn:110,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Tools of rDNA technology",q:`Match List I with List II:
List-I \u2014 List-II
A. Genetically modified organism \u2014 I. Agrobacterium tumefaciens
B. Thermostable DNA polymerase \u2014 II. Bt cotton
C. Ti plasmid \u2014 III. Thermus aquaticus
D. pBR322 \u2014 IV. Escherichia coli`,o:["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III"],c:2,s:`Genetically modified organism: Bt cotton.
Thermostable DNA polymerase: Thermus aquaticus.
Ti plasmid: Agrobacterium tumefaciens.
pBR322: Escherichia coli.`},{id:"neet-2026-q119",pn:119,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Polymerase chain reaction",q:"Identify the correct sequence of steps in each cycle of Polymerase Chain Reaction:",o:["Denaturation \u2192 Annealing \u2192 Extension","Denaturation \u2192 Extension \u2192 Annealing","Extension \u2192 Annealing \u2192 Denaturation","Annealing \u2192 Denaturation \u2192 Extension"],c:0,s:"The correct sequence of steps in PCR is Denaturation \u2192 Annealing \u2192 Extension."},{id:"neet-2026-q120",pn:120,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:`Which of the following statements are correct with respect to DNA separation, isolation and visualization?
A. The cutting of DNA is done by molecular scissors.
B. The DNA fragments separate according to their size in an agarose gel, upon electrophoresis.
C. The separated DNA fragments can be seen without staining when exposed to UV light.
D. The separated DNA fragments, when stained with ethidium bromide, can be seen in visible light.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A and D only","B and D only","B and C only","A and B only"],c:3,s:`DNA is cut by restriction enzymes (molecular scissors), giving DNA fragments.
The fragments are separated by gel electrophoresis.
They separate according to size through the sieving effect of the agarose gel.
They can be seen only after staining with ethidium bromide followed by exposure to UV radiation.
So A and B are correct; C and D are wrong.`},{id:"neet-2026-q127",pn:127,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Somatic hybridisation",q:`Arrange the following steps of somatic hybridisation in a correct sequence.
A. Digestion of cell walls.
B. Isolation of naked protoplasts.
C. Fusion of protoplasts to get hybrid protoplast.
D. Isolation of single cells from two different varieites of plants.
E. Growing of hybrid protoplast to form a new plant.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["E, A, B, C, D","D, A, B, C, E","E, B, A, D, C","D, B, A, E, C"],c:1,s:`Single cells (protoplasts) are isolated from two different varieties of plants, each with a desirable character.
Their cell walls are digested, giving naked protoplasts (surrounded by plasma membrane).
These fuse to give a hybrid protoplast, which is grown to form a new plant.
Such hybrids are somatic hybrids and the process is somatic hybridisation.
Correct sequence: D, A, B, C, E.`},{id:"neet-2026-q136",pn:136,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Cloning vector pBR322",q:"Insertion of a foreign DNA at BamHI site in an E.coli cloning vector pBR322 results in the loss of antibiotic resistance towards:",o:["Gentamycin","Ampicillin and tetracycline","Tetracycline","Ampicillin"],c:2,s:"If a foreign DNA is ligated at the BamHI site of the tetracycline resistance gene in pBR322, the recombinant plasmid loses tetracycline resistance because the gene is interrupted by the foreign DNA."},{id:"neet-2026r-q138",pn:138,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Recombinant DNA technology",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Transformation \u2014 I. Restriction enzyme
B. Cloning site \u2014 II. Transfer DNA to host bacteria
C. Selection \u2014 III. Replication
D. Ori \u2014 IV. Antibiotic
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],c:3,s:`Transformation is transfer of DNA to the host bacteria (A-II).
Cloning site is a DNA segment in a plasmid vector with unique recognition sequences for restriction enzymes (B-I).
Antibiotics help in selection of recombinants (C-IV).
Ori is the DNA sequence where the host's replication machinery starts copying the plasmid (D-III).`},{id:"neet-2026r-q143",pn:143,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"PCR",q:"During PCR, primers bind to the DNA strands in the ______step.",o:["extension","annealing","ligation","denaturation"],c:1,s:`PCR has three steps: denaturation, annealing and extension.
Primers bind to the DNA strands in the annealing step.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q148",pn:148,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Cloning vectors, plasmids",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Plasmids are autonomously replicating DNA.
Statement II : Plasmids are extrachromosomal DNA.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:3,s:`Plasmids are used as cloning vectors in genetic engineering.
Plasmids are autonomously replicating circular extra-chromosomal DNA.
They can replicate inside bacterial cells independent of the control of the chromosomal DNA.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q168",pn:168,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Isolation of genetic material",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A: In recombinant DNA technology, lysozyme is used for disrupting bacterial cells while cellulase is for plant cells.
Reason R: Isolation of genetic material needs disruption of cells.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`DNA is enclosed within membranes, so the cell must be broken open to release it.
Other macromolecules such as RNA, proteins, polysaccharides and lipids are released along with it.
This is done with enzymes: lysozyme (bacteria), cellulase (plant cells), chitinase (fungus).
So both A and R are correct and R explains A.`},{id:"neet-2025-q092",pn:92,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Polymerase chain reaction",q:"Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) amplifies DNA following the equation.",o:["N\xB2","2\u207F","2n + 1","2N\xB2"],c:1,s:`PCR amplifies DNA as per the equation 2\u207F, where n is the number of cycles.
Example: 3 PCR cycles give 2\xB3 = 2 \xD7 2 \xD7 2 = 8 DNA fragments.`},{id:"neet-2025-q115",pn:115,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Selection of recombinants",q:"In the above represented plasmid, an alien piece of DNA is inserted at EcoRI site. Which of the following strategies will be chosen to select the recombinant colonies?",o:["Using ampicillin & tetracycline containing medium plate.","Blue color colonies will be selected.","White color colonies will be selected.","Blue color colonies grown on ampicillin plates can be selected."],c:2,s:`White-coloured colonies will be selected.
The alien DNA is inserted at the EcoRI site, so the \u03B2-galactosidase gene there undergoes insertional inactivation.
This gene gives blue-coloured colonies, but since it is inactivated, white colonies are produced.
The ampicillin and tetracycline resistance genes stay intact, so the DNA will show ampR and tetR.`,fig:{w:318,h:190,alt:"A circular plasmid with Tet R, BamH1, Ori, amp, EcoRI and beta Galactosidase marked around it.",d:[["p","M 36.6 91.2 A 88.2 74.4 0 1 1 213 91.2 A 88.2 74.4 0 1 1 36.6 91.2 Z"],["p","M 109.2 10.8 L 165 13.2 L 192 39 L 184.8 49.2 L 156 40.8 L 112.2 24 Z","f"],["l",114,13.2,118.8,25.2],["l",129,18,133,29.8],["l",144,22.8,147.1,34.3],["l",159,27.6,161.3,38.9],["l",174,32.4,175.4,43.4],["l",189,37.2,189.6,48],["p","M 112.8 19.2 Q 162 21 190.8 43.2","B"],["p","M 25.2 57 L 73.8 67.2 L 73.8 99 L 19.8 118.8 Z","f"],["l",31.2,61.2,28.2,111.9],["l",37.8,62.5,34.8,109.6],["l",44.4,63.8,41.4,107.3],["l",51,65.2,48,105],["l",57.6,66.5,54.6,102.7],["l",64.2,67.8,61.2,100.4],["l",70.8,69.1,67.8,98],["p","M 87 145.8 L 145.2 150 L 145.2 159.6 L 93 162.6 Z","f"],["l",93,147.4,93,160.8],["l",98.4,147.8,98.4,160.8],["l",103.8,148.2,103.8,160.8],["l",109.2,148.6,109.2,160.8],["l",114.6,148.9,114.6,160.8],["l",120,149.3,120,160.8],["l",125.4,149.7,125.4,160.8],["l",130.8,150.1,130.8,160.8],["l",136.2,150.4,136.2,160.8],["l",141.6,150.8,141.6,160.8],["p","M 205.2 73.2 Q 213 114 175.2 145.2"],["p","M 211.2 73.2 Q 218.4 117 182.4 150"],["p","M 208.2 78 Q 216 114 177.6 147.6","B"],["l",219,73.8,232.2,82.8],["l",232.2,82.8,190.8,157.2],["l",181.2,150,190.8,157.2],["l",186,103.2,226.8,103.2],["l",214.8,124.8,231,130.8],["l",69,25.8,69,42],["t",25.2,28.8,"BamH1","s"],["t",169.8,14.4,"Tet R","s"],["t",1.2,91.2,"Ori","s"],["t",229.2,108,"EcoRI","s"],["t",225,141.6,"\u03B2 Galactosidase","s"],["t",100.8,180,"amp","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q126",pn:126,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Bioreactors",q:"Identify the part of a bio-reactor which is used as a foam braker from the given figure.",o:["A","B","D","C"],c:3,s:`Part labelled C is the foam breaker.
B is the motor, A is the flat bladed impeller, D is sterile air inlet.`,fig:{w:210,h:266.5,alt:"Stirred-tank bioreactor with parts A, B, C and D pointed out by arrows.",d:[["p","M 23.4 61.1 V 217.1 Q 23.4 237.9 79.3 237.9 Q 136.5 237.9 136.5 217.1 V 61.1"],["p","M 23.4 61.1 Q 79.3 42.9 136.5 61.1"],["p","M 23.4 74.1 Q 79.3 89.7 136.5 74.1"],["l",79.3,22.1,79.3,235.3,""],["c",79.3,16.9,6.5],["l",13,68.9,23.4,68.9,""],["l",29.9,48.1,29.9,61.1,""],["l",54.6,87.1,104,87.1,""],["l",61.1,79.3,70.2,94.9,""],["l",88.4,79.3,97.5,94.9,""],["l",54.6,104,104,104,""],["l",61.1,96.2,70.2,111.8,""],["l",88.4,96.2,97.5,111.8,""],["l",54.6,149.5,104,149.5,""],["l",61.1,141.7,70.2,157.3,""],["l",88.4,141.7,97.5,157.3,""],["l",54.6,191.1,104,191.1,""],["l",61.1,183.3,70.2,198.9,""],["l",88.4,183.3,97.5,198.9,""],["l",79.3,235.3,79.3,248.3,""],["l",65,248.3,93.6,248.3,""],["l",93.6,237.9,117,237.9,""],["l",117,237.9,117,245.7,""],["l",94.9,41.6,163.8,41.6,"a"],["t",171.6,46.8,"B",""],["l",94.9,70.2,163.8,70.2,"a"],["t",171.6,75.4,"C",""],["l",94.9,101.4,163.8,101.4,"a"],["t",171.6,106.6,"A",""],["l",94.9,243.1,163.8,243.1,"a"],["t",171.6,248.3,"D",""]]},sfig:{w:330,h:266.5,alt:"Bioreactor with B motor, C foam breaker, A flat bladed impeller and D sterile air inlet marked.",d:[["p","M 23.4 61.1 V 217.1 Q 23.4 237.9 79.3 237.9 Q 136.5 237.9 136.5 217.1 V 61.1"],["p","M 23.4 61.1 Q 79.3 42.9 136.5 61.1"],["p","M 23.4 74.1 Q 79.3 89.7 136.5 74.1"],["l",79.3,22.1,79.3,235.3,""],["c",79.3,16.9,6.5],["l",13,68.9,23.4,68.9,""],["l",29.9,48.1,29.9,61.1,""],["l",54.6,87.1,104,87.1,""],["l",61.1,79.3,70.2,94.9,""],["l",88.4,79.3,97.5,94.9,""],["l",54.6,104,104,104,""],["l",61.1,96.2,70.2,111.8,""],["l",88.4,96.2,97.5,111.8,""],["l",54.6,149.5,104,149.5,""],["l",61.1,141.7,70.2,157.3,""],["l",88.4,141.7,97.5,157.3,""],["l",54.6,191.1,104,191.1,""],["l",61.1,183.3,70.2,198.9,""],["l",88.4,183.3,97.5,198.9,""],["l",79.3,235.3,79.3,248.3,""],["l",65,248.3,93.6,248.3,""],["l",93.6,237.9,117,237.9,""],["l",117,237.9,117,245.7,""],["l",94.9,41.6,163.8,41.6,"a"],["t",171.6,46.8,"B - Motor","s"],["l",94.9,70.2,163.8,70.2,"a"],["t",171.6,75.4,"C - Foam breaker","s"],["l",94.9,101.4,163.8,101.4,"a"],["t",171.6,106.6,"A - Flat bladded impeller","s"],["l",94.9,243.1,163.8,243.1,"a"],["t",171.6,248.3,"D - Sterile air","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q136",pn:136,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : The DNA fragments extracted from gel electrophoresis can be used in construction of recombinant DNA.
Statement II : Smaller size DNA fragments are observed near anode while larger fragments are found near the wells in an agarose gel.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both statement I and statement II are correct","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct"],c:0,s:`Cutting DNA with restriction endonucleases gives DNA fragments, which are separated by gel electrophoresis.
Separated DNA bands are cut out of the agarose gel and extracted from the gel piece. This step is elution.
The purified fragments are used to build rDNA by joining them with cloning vectors.
In gel electrophoresis, fragments separate by size through the sieving effect of agarose.
The smaller the fragment, the farther it moves from cathode towards anode.`},{id:"neet-2025-q172",pn:172,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gene cloning enzymes",q:`Which of the following enzyme(s) are NOT essential for gene cloning?
A. Restriction enzymes
B. DNA ligase
C. DNA mutase
D. DNA recombinase
E. DNA polymerase
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["C and D only","A and B only","D and E only","B and C only"],c:0,s:`Gene cloning is a process where a specific gene or DNA sequence is isolated and copied, making many identical copies.
In gene cloning, restriction enzymes, DNA ligase and DNA polymerase are mainly used.`},{id:"neet-2025-q179",pn:179,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Blue-white selection",q:`The blue and white selectable markers have been developed which differentiate recombinant colonies from non-recombinant colonies on the basis of their ability to produce colour in the presence of a chromogenic substrate.
Given below are two statements about this method:
Statement I : The blue coloured colonies have DNA insert in the plasmid and they are identified as recombinant colonies.
Statement II : The colonies without blue colour have DNA insert in the plasmid and are identified as recombinant colonies.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],c:3,s:`Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct.
Recombinant DNA is inserted within the coding sequence of the enzyme \u03B2-galactosidase.
This inactivates the gene for making this enzyme (insertional inactivation).
So colonies with an insert do not produce colour and are identified as recombinant.
Non-recombinant colonies make blue colour in the presence of the chromogenic substrate.`},{id:"neet-2024-q108",pn:108,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction enzymes",q:"Hind II always cuts DNA molecules at a particular point called recognition sequence and it consists of :",o:["4 bp","10 bp","8 bp","6 bp"],c:3,s:`Hind II was the first restriction endonuclease whose working was found to depend on a specific DNA sequence.
It always cuts DNA at a particular point by recognising a sequence of six base pairs.
The other options have more or fewer than 6 bp, so they are wrong.`},{id:"neet-2024-q126",pn:126,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Cloning and gene transfer",q:`What is the fate of piece of DNA carrying only gene of interest which is transferred into an alien organism?
A. The piece of DNA would be able to multiply itself independently in the progeny cells of the organisms.
B. It may get generated into the genome of the recipient.
C. It may multiply and be inherited along with the host DNA.
D. The alien piece of DNA is not an integral part of chromosome.
E. It shows ability to replicate.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["B and C only","A and E only","A and B only","D and E only"],c:0,s:`A piece of DNA carrying only the gene of interest cannot multiply by itself in the progeny cells.
But if it gets integrated into the genome of the recipient, it may multiply and be inherited along with the host DNA.
So B and C are the correct statements.`},{id:"neet-2024-q137",pn:137,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Somatic hybridisation",q:"Which of the following are fused in somatic hybridization involving two varieties of plants?",o:["Protoplasts","Pollens","Callus","Somatic embryos"],c:0,s:"In somatic hybridisation, the protoplasts of two plant varieties are fused."},{id:"neet-2024-q170",pn:170,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Cloning vector pBR322",q:"The following diagram showing restriction sites in E.coli cloning vector pBR322. Find the role of 'X' and 'Y' genes :",o:["The gene 'X' is for protein involved in replication of Plasmid and 'Y' for resistance to antibiotics.","Gene 'X' is responsible for recognition sites and 'Y' is responsible for antibiotic resistance.","The gene 'X' is responsible for resistance to antibiotics and 'Y' for protein involved in the replication of Plasmid.","The gene 'X' is responsible for controlling the copy number of the linked DNA and 'Y' for protein involved in the replication of Plasmid."],c:3,s:`Option (4) is correct.
X is ori, which controls the copy number of the linked DNA.
Y is rop, which codes for a protein involved in plasmid replication.
Options (1), (2) and (3) link X and Y to wrong functions.`,fig:{w:230,h:180,alt:"Circular map of plasmid pBR322 with restriction sites EcoR I, Cla I, Hind III, Pvu I, Pst, BamH I, Sal I and Pvu II marked outside, and genes X and Y marked on lower-left of the ring.",d:[["c",110,92,55],["c",110,92,43],["p","M93.0 39.7 A55 55 0 0 1 132.4 41.8 L127.5 52.7 A43 43 0 0 0 96.7 51.1Z","F"],["p","M132.4 41.8 A55 55 0 0 1 137.5 139.6 L131.5 129.2 A43 43 0 0 0 127.5 52.7Z","f"],["p","M157.6 119.5 A55 55 0 0 1 131.0 142.8 L126.5 131.7 A43 43 0 0 0 147.2 113.5 Z","F"],["p","M131.0 142.8 A55 55 0 0 1 102.8 146.5 L104.4 134.6 A43 43 0 0 0 126.5 131.7 Z",""],["p","M102.8 146.5 A55 55 0 0 1 82.5 139.6 L88.5 129.2 A43 43 0 0 0 104.4 134.6 Z","f"],["p","M82.5 139.6 A55 55 0 0 1 66.4 125.5 L75.9 118.2 A43 43 0 0 0 88.5 129.2 Z","f"],["p","M66.4 125.5 A55 55 0 0 1 56.9 106.2 L68.5 103.1 A43 43 0 0 0 75.9 118.2 Z","F"],["p","M56.9 106.2 A55 55 0 0 1 93.9 39.4 L97.4 50.9 A43 43 0 0 0 68.5 103.1 Z",""],["t",110,78,"pBR322","ms"],["l",38,22,98,40],["t",34,26,"EcoR I","se"],["t",58,12,"Cla I","s"],["l",95,16,95,38],["t",110,14,"Hind III","s"],["l",104,22,104,38],["t",34,50,"Pvu I","se"],["l",38,46,76,60],["t",28,68,"Pst","se"],["l",32,64,70,76],["t",166,48,"BamH I","s"],["l",164,45,142,52],["t",182,92,"Sal I","s"],["l",178,90,163,92],["t",106,170,"Pvu II","s"],["l",110,162,110,148],["t",90,108,"X","m"],["l",86,112,80,121],["t",117,114,"Y","ms"],["l",113,118,113,133]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q178",pn:178,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Bioreactors",q:"Which of the following statements is incorrect?",o:["Bio-reactors are used to produce small scale bacterial cultures.","Bio-reactors have an agitator system, an oxygen delivery system and foam control system.","A bio-reactor provides optimal growth conditions for achieving the desired product.","Most commonly used bio-reactors are of stirring type."],c:0,s:`Statement (1) is incorrect.
Bioreactors process large volumes (100 to 1000 litres) of culture.
Small cultures cannot give appreciable amounts of product, so bioreactors are developed for large-scale production.`}]})();
